    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "thanks. so for the dead load, do i just calculate the weight of the cedar itself or do i need to factor in the railing and the hardware too? also just curious, what are the typical psi requirements for the concrete footings to handle those loads?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "Sorry for any confusion earlier. Yes, when calculating the dead load for your deck, you should include the weight of the cedar, the railing, fasteners, and any other permanent fixtures. Specifically for the western red cedar, you'll need to consider its weight per cubic foot and add the weight of the decking board and any supporting structures.\n\nFor a typical outdoor cantilever deck, the concrete footings should be capable of handling a live load of around 40 pounds per square foot (PSF) and a dead load – including the self-weight of the structure – of approximately 10 to 15 PSF. This generally adheres to VPDCD guidelines, but your design will depend on local building codes and the specifics of your build. For precise calculations, it's wise to consult the relevant building department or a professional engineer, especially for a custom or complex structure like a cantilever deck.",
